<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n14387" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dis-sĕrēno</span></b> ,
			āre, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>v. impers.</i> and <i>a.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Impers.</i>, <i>to be clear</i>, as
			if by dispersing the clouds: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">si cacumina (montium) pura flent, disserenabit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					18, 35, 82, § 356</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> In
			late Lat., <i>act.</i>, <i>to make
			clear.</i>—Only trop., <i>to clear
			away</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">disserena oculis nostris nubilum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">August. Conf. 13, 15</span></i>
			; <i>to cheer</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mores procellosos,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cassiod. Var. 6, 6 <i>init.</i></span></i>
			</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>